def i2c-level-shift
  title "I2C level shifter, two BSS138 with pull-ups on both sides (100 kHz)"
  about "Bidirectional I2C level shifter for Standard mode (100 kHz): per line a BSS138LT1G with its gate on the
      low-side supply, source on the low side, drain on the high side, and 4.7 kΩ pull-ups on both sides. Idle, both
      sides sit at their own supply; a low on the low side turns the MOSFET on, a low on the high side pulls the low
      side down through the body diode until the MOSFET turns on. For 400 kHz or a low side below 2.75 V: a PCA9306
      (notes)."
  port sda_lv "SDA on the low-voltage side (the controller, e.g. a 3.3 V MCU)"
  port scl_lv "SCL on the low-voltage side"
  port v_lv "the low-side supply, 2.75 V or more – it is also the gates' voltage"
  port sda_hv "SDA on the high-voltage side (e.g. 5 V devices)"
  port scl_hv "SCL on the high-voltage side"
  port v_hv "the high-side supply, at least v_lv"
  part Q1 transistor BSS138LT1G lcsc C82045 "{as}: SDA level shift"
  part Q2 transistor BSS138LT1G lcsc C82045 "{as}: SCL level shift"
  part R1 resistor 0805W8F4701T5E lcsc C17673 value 4.7k "{as}: SDA pull-up low side 4.7 k"
  part R2 resistor 0805W8F4701T5E lcsc C17673 value 4.7k "{as}: SCL pull-up low side 4.7 k"
  part R3 resistor 0805W8F4701T5E lcsc C17673 value 4.7k "{as}: SDA pull-up high side 4.7 k"
  part R4 resistor 0805W8F4701T5E lcsc C17673 value 4.7k "{as}: SCL pull-up high side 4.7 k"
  net v_lv Q1.gate Q2.gate R1.1 R2.1
  net v_hv R3.1 R4.1
  net sda_lv @v_lv Q1.source R1.2 bus i2c
  net scl_lv @v_lv Q2.source R2.2 bus i2c
  net sda_hv @v_hv Q1.drain R3.2 bus i2c
  net scl_hv @v_hv Q2.drain R4.2 bus i2c
  covers REQ.missing REQ.value LIM.range/gate
  notes "Why BSS138 and not PCA9306: edakit simulates the BSS138 – an nmos with a SPICE model – so edakit sim checks
      the translation itself (in the example the 5 V side follows: 0.06 V when the controller pulls SDA low, 5 V when
      it lets go) and LIM.range/gate holds the gate drive to the specified on-resistance; the PCA9306 is a pass-gate
      IC with a VREF bias network (200 kΩ, TI SCPS113O section 9) for which edakit has no behaviour model, so its two
      sides would be unconnected nets in the simulation. The discrete circuit is also the one maker-coverage names
      (SparkFun BOB-12009) and needs one new library entry."
  notes "BSS138LT1G (onsemi BSS138LT1/D Rev. 15) rather than the plain BSS138: its RDS(on) is specified at VGS 2.75 V
      (10 Ω max, –40 … +85 °C), so a 3.3 V gate is inside the specification; the plain onsemi BSS138 (BSS138/D Rev. 7)
      specifies 4.5 V and 10 V only, and LIM.range/gate would report a 3.3 V gate. VGS(th) 0.85 … 1.5 V."
  notes "v_lv at least 2.75 V (the gates sit on it): a 1.8 V low side leaves VGS below the specified 2.75 V –
      LIM.range/gate reports it; use a PCA9306 there (VREF1 1.2 V up to VREF2 − 0.6 V, TI SCPS113O), not modelled by
      the simulation."
  notes "Pull-ups 4.7 kΩ on each of the four lines: inside edakit's I2C window (REQ.value: (V − 0.4 V) / 3 mA up to 10
      kΩ, catalog tables/i2c.toml after NXP UM10204 Rev. 7.0, 7.1) on both sides. With tr = 0.8473 · Rp · Cb and
      Standard mode's limit (2.95 kΩ at 400 pF, i.e. about 1 µs; maker-coverage MCU 16) 4.7 kΩ allows about 250 pF per
      side – a small board, not a long cable."
  notes "When the low side pulls a line low it sinks both pull-ups through the MOSFET: (3.3 V − 0.4 V) / 4.7 kΩ + (5 V
      − 0.4 V) / 4.7 kΩ ≈ 1.6 mA, within the 3 mA of Standard mode. edakit checks each side's pull-up alone – 2.2 kΩ
      on both sides would pass REQ.value but sink about 3.4 mA. Fast mode (400 kHz) needs smaller pull-ups than this
      sum allows: PCA9306."
  notes "Each MOSFET adds its capacitance to both lines (Ciss ≤ 50 pF, Coss ≤ 25 pF, Crss ≤ 5 pF at 25 V, more at low
      VDS – datasheet figure 12)."
  notes "Once per bus. Modules with their own pull-ups ('pull': 'up' on their pins) add in parallel on their side; the
      block marks all four nets with \"bus\": \"i2c\" so that REQ.missing and REQ.value check them."
  notes "sim.json: nothing needed for the idle state; to see a low cross over, the controller's SDA pin under its mcu
      outputs with a load at 0 and one at 1 (as the block's test does)."
  set test {
      "connect": {"sda_lv": "SIG", "scl_lv": "SIG2", "v_lv": "3V3", "v_hv": "+5V"},
      "sim": {
        "mcu": {
          "U1": {
            "outputs": ["LED_SDI", "LED_CLK", "LED_LE", "LED_OE", "SIG"],
            "pullups": ["BTN"],
            "pullup_ohm": 50000}},
        "loads": {
          "sda_high": {
            "about": "the controller releases SDA: both sides high",
            "gpio": {"LED_OE": 1, "SIG": 1},
            "leds_on": []}}}}
